WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:08.550
- How much did our argument
against the Clippers drive you

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:10.844
to ultimately leave the Warriors?

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:28.945
K, what's up, man?

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:29.779
I appreciate you

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:30.989
coming on to my show “Chips.”

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:35.035
Obviously it stems from exploring
that chip on your shoulder,

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:38.329
what drives us and makes us who we are

00:00:38.496 --> 00:00:39.748
and why we’re here today.

00:00:39.748 --> 00:00:40.373
So thank you.

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:41.082
- No doubt, man.

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:42.000
Thanks for having me, bro.

00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:46.004
- So take me on the journey: Coach
Brown, your first coach, how was that?

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:49.007
And what did he teach you?
- He taught me fundamentals.

00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:52.844
I mean, it felt like fundamentals of the
game, but it was, like, truly fundamentals of life.

00:00:53.678 --> 00:00:58.308
What it’s gonna take for me to survive
as a human being, a maturing human being.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.644
He put this game in front of me,
told me the fundamentals of it, the

00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:04.022
balance of the game, the history of it.

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:07.776
I just started to understand
that, for one, but also started

00:01:07.776 --> 00:01:08.902
to see life a little bit clearer too.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.655
It was one of those special times in my
life that I always revert back to, you know?

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:17.994
- You hear the cliche saying
of everything you do, do at game speed.

00:01:18.036 --> 00:01:21.414
To me, that is the most
cliche statement in the world.

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:21.748
- Yeah.

00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:25.376
- Except for when it comes to you. You
actually do every rep game speed.

00:01:25.376 --> 00:01:27.796
Does that come from him, or does
that come from somewhere else?

00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:28.213
- Yeah.

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:31.049
Well, he built the foundation,
and the cliche stuff—

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:32.092
I mean, I am cliched.

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:33.760
I mean, I'm simple. I'm efficient.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:34.427
I'm to the point.

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.971
That's what my game is, and all
the best stuff to me is cliche.

00:01:37.514 --> 00:01:40.934
So I just tried to approach every
workout like a game, every game

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:45.271
like a workout.
- And then you go to high school, and you went to

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.649
several different high schools.
- Yeah.

00:01:48.024 --> 00:01:49.901
- How did you feel about that then?

00:01:50.026 --> 00:01:53.738
And looking back on that,
how do you feel about it now?

00:01:53.822 --> 00:01:55.824
- So back then, I didn't
know it was any different.

00:01:56.032 --> 00:02:00.787
I was listening to my—Coach Brown,
my mom, my pops. Like, this was

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:02.038
where the best competition was.

00:02:02.038 --> 00:02:04.499
So I would switch schools
every year to move up a level.

00:02:04.541 --> 00:02:07.377
- People have tried to make
that connection of you

00:02:08.503 --> 00:02:12.382
being at several different high schools to
now you being on three different teams.

00:02:12.507 --> 00:02:15.176
What do you say to that or those people?

00:02:15.677 --> 00:02:15.760
- Yeah,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.763
I mean, I feel as though at
each spot, even in high school,

00:02:18.763 --> 00:02:20.431
my game was growing so fast.

00:02:20.431 --> 00:02:23.685
I needed to be in a different
environment to see how far that I can go.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:23.852
- And,

00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:25.687
and why Texas?

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:27.772
In my opinion, Texas is a football school.

00:02:27.772 --> 00:02:29.858
- Yeah, it is. It is. It's a football state.

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:33.820
But underneath all of that is some
incredible athletes on the basketball

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.697
side. It was this perfect situation.

00:02:35.697 --> 00:02:38.783
And then being in Austin, it was
like, you know, the city itself, you

00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:40.952
know, it wasn't a normal college town.

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:42.370
It was like being in a major city.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:43.371
So it was perfect for me.

00:02:43.746 --> 00:02:48.585
- I know growing up in Saginaw, for me,
being the tallest kid, everybody always

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:50.086
wanted to, wanted me to play center.
- Mmhmm.

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:54.757
- And I was fortunate enough to have
my uncle, who just taught me how to

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:56.593
dribble and taught me guard skills.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.889
But I still always end up throwing
at the center, whereas you're

00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:04.100
seven feet . And you'll never
admit it, but you're seven feet.

00:03:04.475 --> 00:03:08.813
You never played the 4 in your life
till you came to Golden State,

00:03:08.813 --> 00:03:09.189
really.

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:12.233
And when we—
- I played a little bit in college cuz I couldn't dribble

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:14.611
that well, but I was still on
the perimeter most of the time.

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:16.946
- And when we went small,

00:03:17.071 --> 00:03:21.117
that's when you would play the
4. Whose idea was it, like, to

00:03:21.117 --> 00:03:22.952
never just stick you on the block

00:03:22.952 --> 00:03:24.621
cuz you were always
taller than everybody?

00:03:24.746 --> 00:03:25.997
- Coach Brown and then Coach Chucky.

00:03:26.748 --> 00:03:29.751
Like, they would see me every
day, just running up and down the

00:03:29.751 --> 00:03:32.629
court. Like, no ball in my hand,
just running around with kids.

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:35.673
And they seen my agility outside,
running, playing football and stuff.

00:03:35.673 --> 00:03:36.466
So they seen that

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:39.677
I wasn't just a stiff. So I—
- So you callin’ me a stiff? All right, bet.

00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:44.349
- I mean...nah, you’re not a stiff though.
And you're not even big, bro.

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:45.516
You’re like 6’6”.

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:47.769
Like, you play bigger
than what you really are.

00:03:48.353 --> 00:03:50.063
But most guys that's tall and skinny,

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:52.106
they can't really move at the same times.

00:03:52.315 --> 00:03:56.277
And, you know, I always wanted to
be a point guard and shooting guard.

00:03:56.277 --> 00:03:57.695
I was just growing so fast.

00:03:57.695 --> 00:04:00.657
So some coaches did just stick me
underneath the basket, but if I

00:04:00.657 --> 00:04:02.825
get a rebound, I go coast to coast.

00:04:02.867 --> 00:04:03.534
You know what I'm saying?
- Mmhmm.

00:04:03.534 --> 00:04:06.037
- If I—I’d shoot a jump
shot here and there.

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:08.706
So I was showing flashes,
but it wasn't like this.

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:09.582
You know what I'm saying?
- Yeah.

00:04:09.791 --> 00:04:11.167
- I developed this over time.

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:14.462
- I remember reading a
statement where you said,

00:04:15.672 --> 00:04:18.258
“I'm tired of being No. 2.
I was the No. 2 pick.

00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:20.677
I was the No. 2
player in the country.”

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:22.553
Where was that coming from,

00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:23.721
like, and why

00:04:24.472 --> 00:04:31.020
did you feel that?
- Yeah, cuz
we had just lost in the Finals, and I

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:34.649
felt like we was right there every game.
And people was like, “Man, good job.

00:04:35.191 --> 00:04:35.900
Y'all did great.”

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:38.069
Like, “You guys are young,
Y’all will be right back.”

00:04:38.069 --> 00:04:39.696
And I was like, “I don't care about that.”

00:04:39.696 --> 00:04:42.365
I wanted to win that series, and
I wanted to win a championship.

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:43.908
And that's where that came from.

00:04:43.992 --> 00:04:47.996
I was just—people wanted me to
accept being second so much.

00:04:48.204 --> 00:04:51.082
That was never my mentality ever,
always to win the game.

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:52.834
And, you know, I appreciated

00:04:52.834 --> 00:04:53.084
people, like,

00:04:53.835 --> 00:04:56.421
respecting my game. But I was
like, I wanted more for myself.

00:04:56.462 --> 00:05:01.426
And at that point, that's how I was feeling.
- First nine years in OKC,

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:07.682
and then you decide that you leavin’
OKC. And you ultimately decide

00:05:07.849 --> 00:05:12.228
to come to the Warriors, knowing
that the scrutiny would be there,

00:05:12.353 --> 00:05:17.525
knowing that, you know, everybody
would say the Warriors were this great

00:05:17.525 --> 00:05:21.070
team, but you still ultimately made the
decision.

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:24.073
- Why was that? Was that driven by

00:05:24.115 --> 00:05:26.701
“I don't give a fuck what y'all
think, this is good for me”?

00:05:27.118 --> 00:05:31.664
Or was that just, you know, was it
strictly just a basketball decision?

00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:34.959
- It was a basketball decision,
but I knocked down the, like,

00:05:34.959 --> 00:05:36.878
“not giving a fuck ladder” with that too.

00:05:36.878 --> 00:05:37.462
You know what I'm saying?

00:05:37.462 --> 00:05:40.673
It kinda knocked down two birds with
one stone with that. But it was mainly

00:05:41.549 --> 00:05:45.386
I felt like I was the absolute
perfect fit with what you guys

00:05:45.386 --> 00:05:47.847
were doing on both ends of the ball.

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:51.684
And I knew my game had reached
a point where I needed to really

00:05:51.684 --> 00:05:52.685
see what that looked like.

00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:54.937
I thought that was just

00:05:55.772 --> 00:05:56.731
a no-brainer.

00:05:56.731 --> 00:05:58.483
I would do that shit a million times.

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:01.361
And, you know, again, I wouldn't—I
didn't second-guess at all.

00:06:01.402 --> 00:06:04.530
And I knew that's what I wanted
to do when I was going into these

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.700
meetings, but I really just had to
confirm, go through the process.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:11.954
But, like, I knew from watching the
games, just looking at interviews, just

00:06:11.954 --> 00:06:15.333
seeing everything that y’all were about,
like, that's the way I wanted to play.

00:06:15.333 --> 00:06:16.250
And that's the way I needed to go to,

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:19.045
you know, continue to keep
getting better as a player.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:22.298
Once I tunnel visioned on that, I
knew everything would come afterwards,

00:06:22.298 --> 00:06:25.009
and it was just me going
through that experience and seeing how I

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:26.969
was gonna deal with it. And

00:06:27.011 --> 00:06:28.846
It was, it went perfect.

00:06:29.347 --> 00:06:30.848
- You then do three years there.

00:06:30.932 --> 00:06:34.977
Two championships. Would have been
three had you not got hurt.
- Easily.

00:06:34.977 --> 00:06:39.649
- But in my opinion, after Year 2,
you were over it. You were done with it,

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.278
and you were on to the next thing. But
we had the opportunity to three-peat

00:06:44.487 --> 00:06:48.533
and in my mind, what brought you back
to Golden State for Year 3 was

00:06:48.533 --> 00:06:52.161
just the opportunity to three-peat, but
it didn't feel right for you no more.

00:06:52.161 --> 00:06:53.621
Am I right, wrong?

00:06:54.163 --> 00:06:54.747
- That’s interesting

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:58.709
because a lot of people, that's just the
talk around me the last few years, and I get

00:06:59.544 --> 00:07:03.214
what people are saying, because before I
got to Golden State, I was just

00:07:03.214 --> 00:07:08.719
happy-go-lucky. Like, just bright-eyed to the
experience, not knowing what was ahead. By

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:13.141
the first year, I knew exactly what we needed
to do in order for us to win every night.

00:07:13.391 --> 00:07:16.644
I knew what my role was
to the T. Teams before,

00:07:16.644 --> 00:07:18.479
I didn't quite know what my role was

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:20.273
every night. Did I have to be the scorer?

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:23.359
Did I have to be the facilitator
that had to get other guys involved?

00:07:23.359 --> 00:07:24.318
Did I have to make threes?

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:28.406
I knew exactly what my role was, and
I locked on it with so much focus

00:07:28.865 --> 00:07:31.909
and determination to not
fuck around every day.

00:07:32.076 --> 00:07:36.622
You’ve seen it.
- Mmhmm.
- From workouts to practices, shootarounds, film,

00:07:36.664 --> 00:07:39.208
I was super locked. It
made people on the outside.

00:07:39.208 --> 00:07:40.126
look at me like, “Hold on.

00:07:40.168 --> 00:07:41.461
Is he enjoying this?”

00:07:41.586 --> 00:07:42.753
When I dive into something like that

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:44.839
with that level of focus,

00:07:44.881 --> 00:07:46.466
I don't wanna be anywhere else in life.

00:07:46.549 --> 00:07:51.345
That's the most enjoyable experience
I ever had after that first one.

00:07:51.554 --> 00:07:54.807
If that was the case, I wouldn't
have played 78 games that year.

00:07:54.849 --> 00:07:56.642
I would have just waited till
the end of the season and

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:57.768
tried to play in the playoffs.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:58.978
I played every game.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.230
I went hard every day.

00:08:01.939 --> 00:08:05.318
I cared about every matchup, no
matter who was on the floor. Just my

00:08:05.318 --> 00:08:10.615
look didn’t feel as, like, energetic
or open as it was before.

00:08:10.656 --> 00:08:11.365
And I liked that.

00:08:11.365 --> 00:08:14.702
I liked that I was closed off and
focused on my work. I maybe should

00:08:14.702 --> 00:08:17.497
have communicated that with more people who
were interested in knowing what I was

00:08:17.497 --> 00:08:23.044
going through, but I had the most fun
locking in and completing the task.

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:23.669
You know what I'm saying?

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:26.714
Cuz I knew we was gonna win every night.
- Mmhmm.
- And it's rare

00:08:26.714 --> 00:08:27.924
to get to that point as an NBA player.

00:08:28.716 --> 00:08:30.426
- Yeah.
- Knowing you gonna win every night.

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:33.387
So I just tried to focus in and stay
in the moment more than anything

00:08:34.347 --> 00:08:41.062
- And for my, for my own personal,
um, sanity, cuz I've been getting

00:08:41.062 --> 00:08:42.605
my ass kicked ever since you left.

00:08:43.064 --> 00:08:49.737
So just for my own personal
sanity, how much did our argument

00:08:50.488 --> 00:08:53.908
against the Clippers drive

00:08:53.908 --> 00:08:54.325
you

00:08:54.742 --> 00:08:56.285
to ultimately leave the Warriors?

00:08:56.577 --> 00:08:57.912
- It wasn't the argument.

00:08:57.954 --> 00:08:59.497
It was the way that

00:08:59.539 --> 00:09:02.208
everybody, Steve Kerr,
acted like it didn't happen.

00:09:02.333 --> 00:09:06.671
Bob Myers then tried to just
discipline you and think that that

00:09:06.671 --> 00:09:08.422
would put the mask over everything.

00:09:08.756 --> 00:09:12.426
I really felt like that was such
a big situation for us as a group

00:09:12.468 --> 00:09:15.137
the first time we went through
something like that. We had

00:09:15.137 --> 00:09:16.430
to get that shit all out.

00:09:16.514 --> 00:09:17.765
I remember watching “The Last Dance.”

00:09:18.808 --> 00:09:20.977
And when Scottie didn't go into the game,

00:09:21.185 --> 00:09:24.063
the whole team in the locker room
said, “Scottie, that was fucked up

00:09:24.105 --> 00:09:27.483
that you did that.” We needed that.
We just needed to throw all of that shit

00:09:27.483 --> 00:09:28.276
out on the table and say,

00:09:28.276 --> 00:09:32.113
“Yo, Dray, K, like, that was fucked up that
we even had to go through that.

00:09:32.113 --> 00:09:36.492
Let's just wipe our hands of
that and go finish the task.”

00:09:36.492 --> 00:09:37.535
I don't think we did that.

00:09:37.577 --> 00:09:38.786
We tried to dance around it.

00:09:38.786 --> 00:09:41.664
I just didn't like how all of that,
just the vibe between all of that,

00:09:41.664 --> 00:09:43.040
it just made shit weird to me.

00:09:43.457 --> 00:09:44.709
And I'd rather us

00:09:45.793 --> 00:09:47.837
be who we say we are. Family

00:09:47.837 --> 00:09:50.089
first. Communication is key.

00:09:50.131 --> 00:09:51.382
Like, we didn't show that.

00:09:51.382 --> 00:09:52.883
And that's what rubbed me the wrong way

00:09:52.883 --> 00:09:55.469
more than anything.
- When we landed back from L.A.,

00:09:56.178 --> 00:10:00.766
I sat in the—Hazel was sitting in
the car for an hour, 45 minutes.

00:10:01.017 --> 00:10:05.062
They pulled me in that room at
Signature for an hour and 45 minutes.

00:10:05.062 --> 00:10:08.024
And they tried to tell
me, “You need to apologize.”

00:10:09.233 --> 00:10:09.817
And I told them,

00:10:11.402 --> 00:10:14.947
“I'll talk to K, but y'all aren't
going to tell me what I need to say.”

00:10:15.823 --> 00:10:20.161
And, you know, they went on for an hour and 45
minutes talking, saying a bunch of bullshit.

00:10:20.828 --> 00:10:24.999
And ultimately they realized, “All
right, we're not getting through to him.

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:26.542
We're gonna try again in the morning.”

00:10:27.168 --> 00:10:33.507
And so we met the next morning, and
they said, “All right, you slept on it.

00:10:33.549 --> 00:10:34.675
You ready to apologize?”

00:10:35.718 --> 00:10:37.094
And I told them right then and there,

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:40.765
I said, “Y'all about to fuck this up.”

00:10:41.474 --> 00:10:45.561
I said, “The only person that can
make this right is me and K.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:49.857
And there is nothing that y'all can do,
and y'all are going to fuck this up.”

00:10:50.066 --> 00:10:52.193
And in my opinion, they fucked it up.

00:10:52.193 --> 00:10:52.943
- I think so, too.

00:10:53.486 --> 00:10:58.157
And they told me right then
and there, like, “We're gonna

00:10:58.157 --> 00:10:59.450
suspend you for this game.”

00:11:00.368 --> 00:11:00.910
I laughed in

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:02.995
their, literally laughed in their face.

00:11:03.621 --> 00:11:06.707
And Bob said to me, he said, “Wow.

00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:10.670
That was not the reaction I
was looking for or expecting.”

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:13.214
And I said, “Well, either I'mma
laugh in your face, or

00:11:13.214 --> 00:11:14.215
I’mma cuss you the fuck out.

00:11:14.674 --> 00:11:16.425
So, you pick. I'mma choose laugh.

00:11:16.425 --> 00:11:19.929
So I think what you're doing
is funny, and so I'mma laugh.”

00:11:20.429 --> 00:11:25.810
And so it's interesting to hear
you say essentially the same thing

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:32.024
that I told them that day. But,
you know, moving past that,

00:11:32.066 --> 00:11:34.652
kinda along those same lines.

00:11:34.652 --> 00:11:38.656
You've now played with Russ, who I think
has an incredible chip on his shoulder

00:11:38.656 --> 00:11:43.119
that sometimes goes too far;
myself, who has a chip on my

00:11:43.119 --> 00:11:47.998
shoulder that sometimes goes too far;
Kyrie, who has a chip on his

00:11:47.998 --> 00:11:51.419
shoulder that sometimes goes too far;

00:11:51.585 --> 00:11:57.842
and James, who has a chip on his shoulder
that probably sometimes goes too far. And

00:11:57.842 --> 00:11:59.802
playing with all the guys that you’ve
played with,

00:12:00.553 --> 00:12:04.265
that you could say, “Ah, he
probably goes way too far.”

00:12:05.433 --> 00:12:06.934
- We all go too far, bro.

00:12:06.976 --> 00:12:10.604
And we all got major chips on
our shoulders. There’s a million of us.

00:12:10.604 --> 00:12:13.441
Imagine a million people trying to
walk, run into this door right now.

00:12:13.858 --> 00:12:14.942
Somebody would get elbowed,

00:12:15.776 --> 00:12:18.028
kicked, pushed over.

00:12:18.028 --> 00:12:22.658
Like, we all trying to fit into a one-peg
spot. A hundred, millions of us trying

00:12:22.658 --> 00:12:25.286
to fit into these 12, 15 spots on teams.

00:12:25.619 --> 00:12:27.413
So we gotta have that
chip on our shoulder.

00:12:27.413 --> 00:12:28.789
And I always appreciate that.

00:12:28.956 --> 00:12:29.457
Appreciate the

00:12:29.457 --> 00:12:32.752
people that acknowledge that it's
tough to make it to the league.

00:12:33.127 --> 00:12:35.588
And I had to kick a lot of
motherfuckers down to get here.

00:12:36.005 --> 00:12:39.133
I had to shun a lotta my
friends and a lotta that,

00:12:39.133 --> 00:12:40.551
sacrifice a lotta the personal

00:12:41.093 --> 00:12:43.429
shit that I loved as a kid so I can get here.

00:12:43.679 --> 00:12:47.850
So we all, all of us naturally are
underdogs, got chips on our shows.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:49.393
Cuz it's hard to make it to this point.

00:12:49.852 --> 00:12:56.150
But the person that went too far was
always Draymond Green.
- [Laughing]
- He, I mean, to the

00:12:56.150 --> 00:13:00.905
point where I felt like you were tryna
establish that you not playing around.

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:03.574
Like, you not gonna walk over
me, even though I was a second-round

00:13:03.574 --> 00:13:05.034
pick, and I'm undersized.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:09.663
And I'm not as skilled as y'all want me
to be, or I don't play the way y'all

00:13:09.747 --> 00:13:14.126
traditionally like for people my size
to play. I felt that same way about

00:13:14.126 --> 00:13:17.379
Russell Westbrook because in his story.
I felt the same way about James Harden

00:13:17.421 --> 00:13:20.758
cuz I know his story from, like, when
these dudes was kids what they had to go

00:13:21.383 --> 00:13:24.595
through as everyday people, not just
on the court. It's hard to

00:13:24.595 --> 00:13:25.888
make it through life every day,

00:13:25.888 --> 00:13:26.430
you know what I'm saying?
- Mmhmm.

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:28.933
- Where some of these people come from,
you know, so I started understanding

00:13:28.933 --> 00:13:33.604
that about people I'm realizing, like
now I see why they show their ego.

00:13:33.604 --> 00:13:36.857
They show the pride that they have.
They show the chip that they have

00:13:36.857 --> 00:13:39.985
on their shoulder, because it's
hard as shit to make it—
- Facts.
- Every day.

00:13:40.027 --> 00:13:40.736
You know what I'm saying?

00:13:40.736 --> 00:13:44.490
But you pushed it to the limit where it's,
like, it's cool for me to be who I am.

00:13:45.407 --> 00:13:49.662
And be, and not apologize for it all the
Time.
- Mmhmm.
- Because you don't

00:13:49.662 --> 00:13:53.833
mean no harm to anybody, but shit
that you wanted done, you want it done.

00:13:53.874 --> 00:13:55.543
And we all respected that.

00:13:55.543 --> 00:13:58.379
And we all kinda looked up to that
as guys that come into the league and

00:13:58.379 --> 00:14:00.172
wanna fit into this system so bad.
- Mmhmm.

00:14:00.464 --> 00:14:01.048
You know what I'm saying?
- Facts.

00:14:01.674 --> 00:14:03.425
- Like, wanting to be a part of this whole thing.

00:14:03.843 --> 00:14:07.221
Then you realize, you see dudes who
just going by their own little pace.

00:14:07.221 --> 00:14:09.849
And it's like, oh, that's
more so of my rhythm.
- Mmhmm.

00:14:09.849 --> 00:14:10.474
- You know what I'm saying?

00:14:10.474 --> 00:14:15.062
So a lotta the guys that I play with,
from Russ to James to Ky to Steph

00:14:15.062 --> 00:14:18.816
to Klay, they all kinda moved at the
beat of their own drum. That encourages

00:14:18.816 --> 00:14:22.111
a lotta people to do the same shit,
not just basketball players, but people

00:14:22.111 --> 00:14:25.489
in general who just watch us dealing
with everybody every single day.

00:14:25.489 --> 00:14:28.617
I started thinking like that, you know, started
seeing the beauty in all of this shit.
- Mmhmm.

00:14:29.243 --> 00:14:33.998
To watch you early on in your career,
like you said, you was the happy one, you

00:14:33.998 --> 00:14:36.458
was the kid that carried the backpack.

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:40.421
Nike started a backpack line because
you always, and to this day you still

00:14:40.421 --> 00:14:41.714
carry—
- Still do it, yeah.
- The backpack all the time.

00:14:42.172 --> 00:14:44.842
Um, and so there's this common
misconception that you're not

00:14:45.885 --> 00:14:48.095
the kid that carries the backpack anymore.

00:14:48.095 --> 00:14:51.056
I still think you're the kid that
loves basketball and carry a backpack.
- Yeah.

00:14:51.557 --> 00:14:56.228
- But over the course of the years,
your answers to the media, how you

00:14:56.228 --> 00:14:58.022
deal with the media has changed.

00:14:58.689 --> 00:14:59.565
Why is that?

00:14:59.565 --> 00:15:01.358
Is that just who you grew into?

00:15:01.358 --> 00:15:03.986
Is that because of the way
they act with certain shit?

00:15:03.986 --> 00:15:04.695
Where did that?

00:15:05.237 --> 00:15:07.656
- I felt like the media knew more than me.

00:15:07.948 --> 00:15:10.576
I almost had them on a
pedestal of like, they got more

00:15:10.576 --> 00:15:11.911
history, knowledge of the game.

00:15:11.911 --> 00:15:13.537
They more experienced
than me in this area

00:15:14.413 --> 00:15:15.205
of the NBA.

00:15:15.372 --> 00:15:20.210
So once I started to get more experienced
and realized like, oh no, they can never

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:25.507
be what I am.
- Mmhmm.
- Or know what I know. Or
understand it the way I understand it.

00:15:25.883 --> 00:15:28.886
Some of the questions, you know, they
didn't seem so intelligent to me anymore.

00:15:29.053 --> 00:15:31.138
People wanted to praise
me more than I liked or

00:15:31.931 --> 00:15:35.476
hate me more than I liked. Make a
big deal out of so much other shit.

00:15:35.476 --> 00:15:37.728
And I was just like, you know,
this game is simple to me.

00:15:37.728 --> 00:15:41.023
So some of the stuff that y'all asking
really doesn't move me the way it used to.

00:15:41.273 --> 00:15:43.651
I just, you know, started
to see life different,

00:15:43.651 --> 00:15:44.985
I guess, see this game different.

00:15:44.985 --> 00:15:49.949
So, you know, of course my answers
and questions, you know, about what

00:15:49.949 --> 00:15:51.367
goes on is gonna be different too.

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:55.204
So I just chalked that up to
just maturity and experience.

00:15:56.205 --> 00:16:00.751
- And speaking of media, uh, social
media. Obviously we were teammates

00:16:00.751 --> 00:16:05.339
when you, when you got caught with
the burner account.
- [Laughing]
- One thing I love about

00:16:05.339 --> 00:16:09.093
the burner account is everything
that you said on the burner account

00:16:09.134 --> 00:16:11.095
you just started to say
on Kevin Durant's account.

00:16:11.261 --> 00:16:11.595
- Yeah.

00:16:11.845 --> 00:16:14.515
- A lotta people don't like
it as, you know,
- Mmhmm.
- most people

00:16:14.556 --> 00:16:16.392
voice. They don't like how you

00:16:17.351 --> 00:16:22.690
respond to some fans or how you
respond to this, how you are always

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:27.945
tweeting. What do you make of people's
reaction to you responding to fans?

00:16:27.945 --> 00:16:29.571
And if you think something, you send it.

00:16:29.697 --> 00:16:29.905
- Yeah.

00:16:29.947 --> 00:16:30.114
